#limitation(title: [Microaggregate Quantification Has No Validated Reference Standard])[
Every abundance claim in this section rests on an assay that has not been validated against an external reference standard, and the terminology itself is contested.

A Cochrane review of plasmapheresis for the post-COVID-19 condition examined the laboratory basis before it examined the trials @Cochrane2023Plasmapheresis. It judged the term *microclots* incorrect: the particles are amyloid aggregates that include fibrin(ogen), and amyloid is not a constituent of a thrombus. The review identified five laboratory studies, found particles in healthy and diabetic plasma as well as in post-COVID plasma, found three studies that scored particles visually without blinding, and recorded risk-of-bias concerns across the set. It found no study in which patients with the post-COVID-19 condition had undergone plasmapheresis.

The naming follows the same distinction throughout this book. *Microclot* names the hypothesis-level construct, the clot-like particle that is said to obstruct capillaries. *Microaggregate* names the object a study actually counts or measures, an amyloid fibrin(ogen) particle. The two words are not interchangeable, and a sentence that uses one for the other overstates what was measured.

Three consequences follow for reading this section. First, *presence is not excess*: particles occur in healthy plasma, so a positive finding requires a quantified contrast against a matched control, not detection alone. Second, visual scoring without blinding is open to reader drift, and the review records risk-of-bias concerns across the set rather than any calibration against a fixed standard. Third, without a reference material there is no way to place an abundance value from one laboratory on the same scale as a value from another, so a positive study and a negative study cannot be compared numerically.

The limitation is symmetric. It weakens the positive abundance reports and the null report equally and favours neither. A calibrated, blinded assay with a defined positive control is the precondition for treating microaggregate abundance as a biomarker at all.

*Consequence:* Until such an assay exists, neither a positive nor a negative microaggregate result can be acted on clinically, so microaggregate testing should not be offered to patients and should not be used to select them for anticoagulation or plasmapheresis.
] <lim:microclot-quantification-unvalidated>
